      {
        "id": "GeomagTravel",
        "type": "project",
        "name": "Geomagnetic Travellogue",
        "layout_type": "grid",
        "date": "2025-11-02",
        "write_up": "This project follows the zero-degree line of the\nearth's magnetic field from the year 1590.\nIt is a work in progress.\n\nThe year 1590 is significant, as it coincides with the\ndevelopment of Galileo's first telescope, and with the\ncreation of the first compound microscope.\n\nTherefore, my project explores incidences of macro-\nand micro-scopic vision, magnetic attraction and\npolarity, and the intersection of scientific, historical,\nand artistic narratives.\n\nOn May 10, 2008 I made my first journey. I would attempt to go to Peru, Iceland, South Africa, and Australia.\n\nI started my expedition in Los Angeles, and after carefully studying my maps, I plotted my destinations. I had 24 hours to make this trip.\n\n*parallel worlds*\\\n*using the wrong map to get to the right places*\\\n*can I reach Peru through a looking glass?*",
        "synopsis": "Tracing the zero-degree line of the earth's magnetic field from 1590 to the present, in four journeys.",
        "thumbnail": "/artworks/GeomagTravel/declination.jpg",
        "header_image": "/artworks/GeomagTravel/declination-animated.gif",
        "children": [
